{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#155","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#155","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#155\",\"parent_revision\":\"aea36915af8640819e15dd11d20d97540b23c2ba212db7b450e9611b24923bea\",\"root_revision\":\"aea36915af8640819e15dd11d20d97540b23c2ba212db7b450e9611b24923bea\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: \\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: \\n-->\\n\\n## The Pattern\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nA suppression is justified by a claim about the world. The suppression takes effect immediately; the claim is never checked. Because a suppression's failure mode is **silence**, nothing downstream ever contradicts it.\\n\\nThis is distinct from the claim-drift class already tracked (#124, #130, #131, #133, #136, #146). Those produce a *wrong output* that something eventually catches \u2014 AS007 was caught, #130 was caught, #136 was caught. A false suppression produces *no output*. There is nothing to catch.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Confirmed Instance\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n`.agents/skills/*` was excluded from ruff and ty, justified as \\\"Skills vendored from github.com/jamie-bitflight/claude_skills and maintained there.\\\"\\n\\nNo sync exists in either direction \u2014 no script, no CI job, no `.gitmodules`, and `claude_skills` has no mechanism to distribute skills out. Both skills were added here in #119 and have never been synced from anywhere.\\n\\nTwo first-party files went unlinted for months. A real `D417` (#143) sat in one and was found only because a second repo happened to lint the same file. The claim was then cited in #143 and #135, which made it appear corroborated when both were downstream of the same unverified sentence.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Second Instance Already Filed\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n`packages/skilllint/tests/test_e2e_packaging.py:7`:\\n\\n> Tests are marked with @pytest.mark.slow to avoid running on every pytest invocation\\n\\n`addopts` never passes `-m \\\"not slow\\\"`. The claim describes an intent the configuration does not implement \u2014 10 wheel-build/venv-create tests run on every local invocation, costing 23-45s (#148).\\n\\nSame shape: a claim about configured behaviour, no check that the configuration matches.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Scope Of The Guard Being Built\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nA registry + test covering `[tool.ruff].exclude` and `[tool.ty.src].exclude` \u2014 every exclusion declares a reason from a closed set, and any reason asserting external provenance must name an artifact that exists on disk.\\n</div>\\n\\n## What That Guard Does Not Cover The Point Of This Issue\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n1. **Inline suppressions.** `# noqa`, `# ty: ignore`, `@pytest.mark.skip`/`xfail` carry the same warrant-shaped claims and are unchecked.\\n2. **Prose claims about configuration.** The `slow`-marker case above lives in a module docstring, not in config. Nothing connects a docstring's claim to the `addopts` that would implement it.\\n3. **Named-but-wrong artifacts.** The guard checks that a named `sync` artifact *exists*, not that it does what is claimed. Pointing at a script that does not actually sync would pass.\\n4. **Plausible-but-false reasons inside an allowed category.** A wrong-but-well-formed justification still passes.\\n</div>\\n\\n## The Generalisable Rule\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nThe machine-checkable form of \\\"is this claim true\\\" is \\\"**does the claim name something that must exist, and does it exist**\\\". Claims phrased without a verifiable referent cannot be checked at all \u2014 which is an argument for requiring justifications to name artifacts rather than describe intentions.\\n\\nWorth deciding whether to extend the same registry-plus-existence-check to inline suppressions, or to accept that surface as unguarded and say so explicitly.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- #143 \u2014 the D417 the false claim hid\\n- #135 \u2014 urgency built on the same false claim; corrected\\n- #148 \u2014 the `slow`-marker instance\\n- #112, #136, #146 \u2014 the adjacent claim-drift class\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"30e19fbd7ad767ca6127707c025db3dbfec17e08e9ef5115baa7c5ee89ea36ff\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDORXxKvc8AAAABXP-qSA\"}"}